        "problem": "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}=1.$",
        "worked_solution": "To find the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot take the square root of a negative number. Therefore, the equation has no real solutions. So, there are no $x$-intercepts.",
